% ch-03-ex-02-defined-guards.glp
%
% This file extends the ex-01 §3.2 curriculum: ex-01 used built-in guards only
% (`>` from producer/2, `ground` from consumer/3). ex-02 introduces DEFINED
% guards from book §3.2 (p 22). A defined guard is a unit clause or short
% procedure that the compiler unfolds at guard sites — extending the guard
% vocabulary beyond the built-in set while remaining bound by SRSW
% reader-position rules.
%
% channel/1 + process/2 byte-exact from "The Art of Grassroots Logic
% Programming" (Shapiro, 2025), §3.2, p 22 (PDF p 34). Per `research.md`
% R-008, handle/1 is defined locally as a minimal stub (the book leaves
% handle/1 undefined as a placeholder for downstream processing); the local
% stub preserves byte-exactness of process/2 and provides a tautological
% body call so the goal `process(ch(a, b), Status).` succeeds with
% `Status = ok` per the spec FR-009 + Clarifications Q2 locked binding.
%
% This file is STAND-ALONE per `research.md` R-009: no Program 3.1 / merge/3
% duplication is required because the locked primary goal does not exercise
% merge/3. Loading this file alone in a fresh REPL session is sufficient to
% run the four-goal ex-02 trace (primary + 3 inspection goals).
%
% Compare with ex-03 (next exercise): ex-03 introduces the `~(...)` guard
% negation form on `=?=`, which is a NEGATABLE built-in guard. Defined guards
% (like channel/1 here) are NOT negatable per the §3.2 SRSW Rules table on
% book p 24 — ex-03 demonstrates this distinction via lookup/3.

%% Defined guard: channel-shaped term test (book p 22). Unit clause matching
%% any term of shape `ch(_, _)`. The compiler unfolds this at any guard site
%% that calls channel(X?), turning it into a structural pattern match.
channel(ch(_, _)).

%% process/2 first clause: defined-guard dispatch (book p 22). The guard
%% `channel(X?)` succeeds when X is ch-shaped, binding the caller's Status
%% writer to the literal `ok` (head pattern). Body call `handle(X?)` invokes
%% the local handle/1 stub below.
process(X, ok)    :- channel(X?) | handle(X?).
%% process/2 second clause: otherwise fallback (book p 22). The built-in
%% `otherwise` guard succeeds when all earlier clauses' guards have failed
%% (not suspended). Binds Status to `error` and the body is just `true`.
process(_, error) :- otherwise   | true.

%% handle/1 local stub (per research.md R-008). The book uses `handle(X?)` in
%% process/2's body as a placeholder for downstream processing; ch3 ex-02's
%% pedagogy is the defined-guard machinery, not handle's behaviour, so a
%% tautological unit clause is sufficient. handle(_) succeeds for any single
%% argument, making process/2's first clause body trivially succeed.
handle(_).
